---
/**
 * Twenty minutes of writing that states the entire differentiator plainly.
 *
 * It's also the signal that gets a small site cited by an AI rather than
 * retrieved and discarded — models weight sourcing and provenance, and this
 * page is the provenance statement. Linked from the footer of every page.
 */
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";

const schema = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: "How we verify",
    description:
      "Every fact on Delaware Field Guide carries the date it was checked and the source it came from. Here is the method, and what happens when we get something wrong.",
    mainEntityOfPage: new URL("/how-we-verify/", Astro.site).href,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Delaware Field Guide" },
    publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Delaware Field Guide" },
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "Where do the rules on this site come from?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "From the body that sets them — the city, the state park, or the property itself — by phone or from its own published notice. Never from another guide.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What does the verification date mean?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "It is the date we last read or confirmed that fact with its source. It is not the date the page was published or edited. Pages are labeled either Confirmed, meaning we called or visited, or Read from the source, meaning it came from a named primary source but has not yet been confirmed by phone.",
        },
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        name: "What happens when a fact turns out to be wrong?",
        acceptedAnswer: {
          "@type": "Answer",
          text: "We fix it, re-date it, and say on the page that it was corrected and when. Corrections are not quietly edited away.",
        },
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How I check this — Delaware Field Guide"
  description="Every fact here carries the date it was checked and the source it came from. The method, in full, including what happens when I get something wrong."
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="wrap pt-10 pb-2">
    <p class="label m-0">Method</p>
    <h1 class="text-[2.5rem] sm:text-[3.3rem] mt-3.5 mb-0 max-w-[13ch] text-marsh">How I check this</h1>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap prose-field pb-4">
    <p class="text-[1.2rem] leading-[1.5] font-medium max-w-[58ch]">
      I call whoever sets the rule, write down the date, and publish it when I
      get something wrong.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      My name is Matt Werth. I'm a software engineer and a photographer, I live
      in north Wilmington, and I drive south a lot. Usually with kids in the
      car.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this page exists</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Most guides to the Delaware beaches copy their dog rules off each other.
      That works fine until a town changes one. Then the error spreads across a
      dozen sites and just sits there, because nobody re-checked and nobody had
      a source to re-check against.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Rehoboth moved its seasonal dog dates in January 2026. A lot of what
      you'll find still prints the old ones.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That's not a small thing. It's the difference between a good weekend and
      driving two hours to a beach your dog isn't allowed on.
    </p>

    <h2>The rules</h2>

    <h3>1. The source is whoever sets the rule</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      A town's dog rules come from the town. Either by phone or from its own
      published notice. A state park rule comes from DNREC. A pet fee comes
      from the property itself, not from a booking site's summary of the
      property. I don't take facts from other guides, including when they're
      probably right.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Every fact carries the date I checked it</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Not the date the page went up. Not the date I last touched the file. The
      date I last confirmed the thing with its source. That's what the little
      mono stamp at the bottom of each page is for.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      It tells you how much to trust the page, which is something you can't get
      from a page that doesn't have one.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Every page says how solid the fact is</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There are two honest answers to "how do you know that", and they're not
      the same, so they get labeled separately instead of blurred together.
    </p>

    <dl class="sign breakout my-6 divide-y-2 divide-tide/30">
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label text-flag-green-bearing">Confirmed</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          I called, visited, or asked the people who set the rule. The stamp
          names them and gives the date.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="px-4 sm:px-5 py-4">
        <dt class="label">Read from the source</dt>
        <dd class="m-0 mt-1.5 text-[0.95rem] leading-[1.55] max-w-[58ch]">
          Taken from a named primary source. The city's own notice, DNREC's
          published fee schedule, a county park listing. Accurate as far as it
          goes, and not the same as having asked. The page links it so you can
          check for yourself.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Both get published. The difference is on the page, because "the city
      publishes this" and "I called the city and asked" are different claims and
      you should get to know which one you've got.
    </p>

    <h3>4. What never goes in</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Anything whose only origin is another guide, a review site or a tourism
      write-up. No stamp, marked <code class="stamp">noindex</code>, kept out of
      the sitemap and off the lists until somebody actually checks it.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      That rule has teeth, and it costs me. The dog park pages aren't in search
      yet for exactly this reason. The details came off review sites, and until
      someone stands in each one with a notebook I'm not willing to call them
      facts. The build enforces it, so a draft can't ship as a fact by accident.
    </p>

    <h3>5. "Not confirmed" is a real answer</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Where I don't know, the page says so instead of guessing or quietly
      leaving it out. A blank field means I haven't checked it. It doesn't mean
      the park doesn't have the thing.
    </p>

    <h3 id="corrections">6. I publish the mistakes</h3>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      When I get something wrong I fix it, re-date it, and leave a note saying
      what it used to say and when it changed. <a href="/changed/">There's a log
      of all of it</a>. A site that promises checking and then quietly rewrites
      its errors is worth less than one that never promised.
    </p>

    <h2>Three I caught before they shipped</h2>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      All three the same shape. A rule changed, the change was published
      properly, and nobody re-checked.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Rehoboth re-cut its dog dates in January 2026. State park entry fees
      roughly doubled in March. And seven surf fishing crossings quietly dropped
      their reservation requirement back in 2025.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Every one of those is still printed the old way somewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What I don't do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No live water-quality or bloom tracker. Conditions change by the hour
        and a stale "safe today" badge is worse than no badge at all. I link to
        DNREC and the Division of Public Health, who actually monitor it.
      </li>
      <li>
        No rule I've only seen on an aggregator, however many aggregators agree.
        Agreement between copies isn't corroboration. It's just copying.
      </li>
      <li>
        No payment to change or soften a listing. Lodging links may earn a
        commission one day. The fees, weight limits and policies here are what
        the property told me, and an unfavorable answer gets published exactly
        like a favorable one.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Found something wrong?</h2>
    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      Tell me and I'll check it. People who live here know these rules far
      better than I do, and one note from someone standing on the boardwalk
      yesterday beats another phone call from me.
    </p>

    <p class="max-w-[58ch]">
      There's a report box at the foot of every page, or reach me through
      <a href="https://werth.design" rel="noopener">Werth Design</a>, which is
      my studio and which pays for this.
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
